Comparing VR and Non-VR Driving Simulations: An Experimental User Study

Florian Weidner∗

Anne Hoesch†

Sandra Poeschl‡

Wolfgang Broll§

Ilmenau University of Technology, Germany

ABSTRACT
Up to now, most driving simulators use either small monitors or large immersive projection setups like 2D/3D screens or a CAVE. The recent improvements of VR-HMDs led to an increased application in driving simulation. However, the inﬂuence and comparability of various VR and non-VR displays has been hardly investigated.
We present results of a user study investigating the different inﬂuence of non-VR (2D, stereoscopic 3D) and VR (HMD) on physiological responses, simulation sickness, and driving performance within a single driving simulator. In the study, 94 participants performed the Lane Change Task.
Results indicate that a VR-HMD leads to similar data as stereoscopic 3D or 2D screens. We observed no signiﬁcant difference regarding physiological responses or lane change performance. However, we measured signiﬁcantly increased simulator sickness in the VR-HMD condition compared to stereoscopic 3D.
Keywords: Virtual Reality, Driving Simulation, Human Factors, Simulator Sickness.
Index Terms: H.5.1 [Information Interfaces and Presentation]: Multimedia Information Systems—Artitﬁcal, augmented, and virtual realities H.1.2 [Information Systems ]: User/Machine Systems —Human factors
1 INTRODUCTION
Driving simulators have been an important tool for academia and research. Advantages of driving simulations compared to real driving experiments are safety, controllability and reproducibility [2]. Visualization of the environment is an essential part in driving simulation. In the past, large projection systems like 2D/3D screens or CAVEs have been widely used [4]. Due to recent the technological advances of VR-HMDs, setups including these devices become increasingly popular (e.g. [7]).
However, the application of various display types in research pose a challenge: The question is, if results gathered in non-VR settings are comparable to results from a VR setup. In this area, some research has been done. Forster et al. compared 2D and stereoscopic 3D (S3D) simulations. They investigated lane keeping, distance estimation and simulator sickness. They found that S3D led to different (but not worse) lane keeping behavior and found no difference in distance estimation. However, in the S3D condition, simulator sickness was signiﬁcantly higher [3]. Aykent et al. investigated the difference between 2D and VR-HMD regarding simulator sickness. They concluded that the VR-HMD induces signiﬁcantly more simulator sickness but did not mention results about driving
