The study presented in this paper aims to investigate how different display types (2D, S3D, and VR-HMD) inﬂuence simulator sickness, driving performance, and physiological responses of participants performing the Lane Change Task within a single driving simulator environment.
2 SYSTEM OVERVIEW
For driving simulation, we developed Car2Simulate with Unreal Engine 4. It connects to a Barco F50 120Hz WQXGA projector capable of 2D and active S3D. It illuminates a screen (3.6m × 2.25m) in rear-projection mode (see ﬁg. 1). In the stereo condition, we used volfoni EDGE RF glasses and a volfoni ActivHub RF-50. For the VR condition, an Oculus Rift DK2 (FOV: 100◦ (nominal)) was connected. (see ﬁg. 2). In order to provide the feeling of “sitting in a car”, participants sat in a car mock-up (see ﬁg. 3). It is equipped with a Thrustmaster TX Racing Wheel Leather Edition (incl. pedals), a Thrustmaster TH8A shifter, and a large-scale S3D spatial AR dashboard (see ﬁg. 4). The ﬁnal conﬁguration in 2D and 3D provides a FOV of 60◦ × 35◦. Simulation runs on a workstation equipped with an Intel Xeon CPU E5-2670 v3 with 2.3GHz, an NVIDIA Quadro M6000, and 64GB Ram. The driving simulation runs with 120Hz in 2D, 60Hz per eye in 3D, and 75Hz in VR with the DK2. A 5.2 audio system provides surround sound.

