the biofeedback system (PsyExpert Biofeedback 2000x−pert ). For the next approximately 10 minutes, they drove along a training track to get familiar with the simulator and changing lanes. During the ﬁnal 20 minutes, they performed the Lane Change Task with 97 lane changes. After that, they were disconnected from the biofeedback system and ﬁlled out the Simulation Sickness Questionnaire. The sample consisted of N = 94 university students acquired via university mailing lists (74.5% male, 25.5% female; age: M = 24.77, SD = 4.74).

4 MEASURES
Driving performance was measured by correct lane changes in a shortened version of the Lane Change Task (LCT, [6]). Participants had to drive along a road with three lanes and had to change lanes according to street signs. A lane change is correct if the cars position before a sign conﬁrms with the lane indicated by the previous sign. For physiological responses, we measured electromyography, heart rate variability (HRV), skin conductance, body temperature, blood volume pulse, 3D acceleration of the left and right hand, and respiration frequency. Simulator Sickness was measured according to the Simulator Sickness Questionnaire (SSQ, [5]).

