5.3 LCT Performance Figure 6 illustrates mean LCT scores and standard deviations for each condition (2D: M = 96.66, SD = 1.06; S3D: M = 91.18, SD = 16.37; VR-HMD: M = 96.35, SD = 1.65). A one-way ANOVA indicates signiﬁcant differences between conditions (F(2, 91) = 3.06; p = 0.041). However, a post-hoc Bonferroni analysis does not conﬁrm this (2D - 3D: SE = 2.42; p = 0.08, 2D - VR-HMD: SE = 2.27; p = 1.00, 3D - VR-HMD: SE = 2.29; p = 0.08).
6 DISCUSSION We detected no differences between conditions with respect to physiological responses. A reason for this might be that the rather relaxed nature of the LCT is not exciting enough for measurable

Figure 6: Mean LCT score and standard deviation by condition.
differences. Further, we observed no signiﬁcant differences in driving performance. However, in a scenario where depth perception is more important (e.g. keeping distance to a leading vehicle), this result might change because of the depth cues offered by VR and S3D. Finally, compared to the S3D condition, the Oculus Rift DK2 induced signiﬁcantly more simulator sickness. This difference is only based on self-reported data and is not backed by the physiological responses. We believe that the increased simulator sickness in the VR-HMD condition is because of the HMD’s hardware limitations regarding tracking, resolution, and refresh rate. Limitations of the study are the not highly realistic vehicle physics of the simulation and the not up-to-date VR-HMD. Further, the sample consisted mostly of young students.
7 CONCLUSION AND FUTURE WORK
Results of the study indicate that, for simple tasks, display types do not inﬂuence driving performance and physiological responses signiﬁcantly. Results from studies using these display types and simple tasks seem to be comparable. In order to generalize results, future work should include studies with more complex tasks. In addition to that, it seems to be worth investigating how participants perform while wearing a more up-to-date VR-HMD.
